<file path="src/app/features/developers/developers.page.scss">
// src/app/features/developers/developers.page.scss

// ── Hero ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
/* A gradient band closed by `wave-drip`'s cream swell, carrying one line of copy.
   It used to hold the vendor illustration in a white rounded card plus the page's
   h1 and tagline; the heading moved out to .dev-intro and the illustration went
   entirely.

   The illustration went rather than losing just its card, because the card was
   never doing the job its comment claimed. The SVG painted its own opaque
   full-bleed background (`<rect width="320" height="200" fill="url(#dv-sky)">`)
   and `.vendor-art` was `width: 100%`, so it covered the card completely —
   `background: var(--color-white)` underneath was never visible on screen. The
   card only supplied rounded corners, a shadow and clipping. So there was no white
   to remove; dropping the card alone left the SVG's hard square corners sitting on
   the gradient, and making the SVG transparent instead deleted six of its ten
   elements, measured against both ends of the gradient:

     canopy #5fd9b4    1.03 vs powder   1.00 vs mint   <- identical to the gradient
     apron #8ecae6     1.00 vs powder   1.03 vs mint   <- identical to the gradient
     cap band #8ecae6  1.00 vs powder   1.03 vs mint   <- identical to the gradient
     shirt/cone #ffc53d 1.13            1.10
     scoop #f8a9be     1.03            1.06
     strokes #1b5e7e   3.98            4.09           <- survived
     wheels #3d2b45    7.20            7.41           <- survived

   Only the strokes and the wheels would have remained. */
.dev-hero {
  background: var(--gradient-brand);
  /* Height is `min-height`, not `height`. At 300px with one line of copy in it the
     band is sized deliberately rather than derived from content, and `min-height`
     is what keeps that reversible: add content and the band grows instead of
     clipping it. It also stops a font-metric change from silently resizing the
     hero out from under the swell.

     `box-sizing: border-box` is stated rather than inherited. Nothing in this
     project's own SCSS sets it globally (only app.component.scss and
     cart-button do, locally), so the value was being decided by whatever Ionic's
     core stylesheet happens to apply. Under content-box the same declaration gives
     a 358px band instead of 300px — a silent 19% difference from a number that
     looks identical in the source.

     Measured, not estimated: the swell owns the bottom 27px at the centre, so
     300px of band shows 273px of gradient there and 286px at the edges. Padding
     stays at 28/24/30 purely as the swell's breathing room; raising the band means
     changing this one number and nothing else here moves. Past roughly 340px the
     first card starts falling below the fold on a 390x844 phone, which is where
     this stops being a band and starts being a page-eater.

     `justify-content: center` is the safety property, not a stylistic flourish.
     This is a flex column, so the copy's vertical position decides its distance
     from the swell, and the swell reaches 27px up at the centre where the copy is
     centred. Bottom-aligning it (`flex-end`) would put the text's box 30px above
     the band's bottom edge — a 3px gap, which is the same near-collision that
     `.dev-hero-sub` had before the padding went to 48px. Centred in a 242px
     content box it clears the swell by roughly 100px.

     That clearance is a function of how much content the hero holds, not of the
     line count: `distance = 30 + max(0, 242 - contentHeight) / 2`, so it is safe
     while total hero content stays under about 168px and collapses below a 40px
     margin past 242px. Past 242 the hero grows to `58 + contentHeight` and the
     gap pins at exactly 30px — 3px of clearance — no matter how much more is
     added. So the guard to watch when editing this hero is its total height
     staying at 300px; if it ever exceeds that, the swell is the problem and no
     amount of tuning the tagline will fix it. */
  box-sizing: border-box;
  min-height: 300px;
  /* Bottom padding is 30px, and it is NOT clearance for the copy. The --space-7
     (48px) that briefly replaced it existed to clear `.dev-hero-sub`, which used
     to be this hero's last child at 30px — a 2px gap under a swell that reaches
     27px up at the centre. That text is now `.dev-intro-sub`, on cream. What
     cleared this hero's copy before was `justify-content: center` below, and that
     is what clears it now; the 30px is the swell's own breathing room.

     Geometry, corrected: .wave-drip's ::after is `height: 28px` with
     `bottom: -1px` inside an `overflow: hidden` parent, so the visible peak is
     27px, not 28px. An earlier note here said 28px/20px; the real figures are
     27px/21px. Understated, so harmless, but the arithmetic should be right. */
  padding: 28px 24px 30px;
  text-align: center;
  // Plum, not white — see the header note in variables.scss. This is a
  // light-to-light pastel gradient, so white text would sit at 1.79:1.
  // Measured across the whole 135deg sweep for the tagline, which now sits here:
  // plum is 7.20:1 at the powder end rising to 7.41:1 at mint, so it clears AA at
  // any size on any part of the band. If a background image ever lands behind
  // this, that stops being true and the About hero's 70% white scrim becomes the
  // reference — its own comment has the measured ladder (50% -> 2.75:1 FAIL,
  // 70% -> 4.61:1 PASS).
  color: var(--color-ink);
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: center;
  /* Load-bearing: see the note above. Without it the copy sits at flex-start and
     the 30px bottom padding looks like it is doing the clearing. With it, the
     line is centred in the 242px content box, roughly 100px clear of the swell. */
  justify-content: center;
  gap: 10px;
}

/* The motto: one line in --font-display (Fredoka), the app's own display face and
   the one already on 27 other elements. Nothing decorative about it — no script,
   no brush, no offset shadow, no outline. Every one of those was tried here and
   removed; the reasons live in the template comment and in variables.scss.

   Fredoka's advance on THIS string, measured by rendering it at 200px and
   dividing: 3300.6px over 36 characters = 0.4584em per character. So one line
   needs 16.5em of width, and with -0.3px tracking across 35 gaps it only fits
   once the viewport is past about 346px. That is the whole reason this clamps
   where it does.

   The size is FLUID, which the earlier fixed sizes could not be: that was because
   the poster stack had to fit a fixed 242px content box, and a vw term would only
   have grown it into the same box while adding wrap risk. A single line has 122px
   of clearance at the 48px cap, so there is vertical room to spare and the type
   can scale with the screen.

   Where one line actually holds, at this clamp:
     346px viewport and up  -> ONE LINE
     320px                  -> 286px of text in a 272px box, so it wraps

   320px is the only width that wraps, and `text-wrap: balance` splits it into two
   even lines there rather than stranding "masterpiece." alone. Getting a literal
   single line at 320px would need 17.4px, which is legible here (the fill measures
   7.20:1, far above the 4.5:1 that applies at any size) but is not a size worth
   setting a motto in. */
.dev-hero-tagline {
  font-family: var(--font-display);
  /* A REAL 700, and that is verifiable rather than assumed. Lobster Two and
     Kaushan Script both shipped 400 only, so asking them for 700 produced
     SYNTHESISED bold that smeared the outlines — the reason neither face is on
     this hero any more. Fredoka's loaded face list contains both 500 and 700, so
     this is a cut between two real faces. Note that width is NOT a usable test
     here: those two weights render only 0.5% apart on this string, so a
     "widths must differ" check passes or fails on rounding noise rather than on
     weight. The face list is the honest evidence. */
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: clamp(1.125rem, 5.2vw, 3rem); /* 18px phone floor - 48px desktop cap */
  /* 1.15 rather than the inherited 1.2: this is one display line, so the slight
     tightening keeps it reading as a single gesture. */
  line-height: 1.15;
  letter-spacing: -0.3px;
  color: var(--color-ink);
  margin: 0;
  /* Only does anything below ~346px, where it evens the two lines instead of
     leaving a stub. Browsers without support (pre-Chrome 114, pre-Safari 17.5,
     pre-Firefox 121) fall back to greedy wrapping rather than breaking. */
  text-wrap: balance;
}

// ── Intro ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
/* Type-matched to the card area it introduces, not to the hero it replaced.
   Same display face and weight as .dev-name (16px), scaled up because a section
   heading should outrank the name it heads; same centring as .dev-card-content;
   --container-max and auto margins exactly as .about-body uses them, so the block
   aligns with the card columns on a wide screen instead of floating wider. */
.dev-intro {
  /* `width: 100%` is load-bearing, not tidiness. `ion-content::part(scroll)` is a
     flex column (global.scss), and on a flex item an `auto` inline margin cancels
     the default `align-self: stretch` — the box shrinks to fit-content and the
     auto margins just centre it. Without the width it collapsed to 256px, the
     intrinsic width of a short heading, so the block no longer lined up with the
     card columns at all.

     `.about-body` gets away with the same `max-width` + `margin: 0 auto` only
     because its children are paragraphs whose max-content width is enormous, so
     fit-content always trips the 1080px cap. A short heading does not, which is
     exactly what made this invisible by comparison. Stating the width makes the
     behaviour depend on the container rather than on how long the text is. */
  width: 100%;
  max-width: var(--container-max);
  margin: 0 auto;
  padding: var(--space-5) var(--space-4) var(--space-3);
  text-align: center;
}

.dev-intro-title {
  font-family: var(--font-display);
  font-size: 24px;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--color-ink);
  margin: 0;
  line-height: 1.25;
}

/* --color-ink-soft on cream is 6.20:1 with the high-contrast ink (#6B5875) and
   9.93:1 with the default one, where on the gradient it was 3.58:1 and 5.80:1.
   The 3.58:1 case failed AA at 14px, so this move fixes it rather than trading
   it. check:contrast cannot see this: it reads a hardcoded palette table. */
.dev-intro-sub {
  font-size: 14px;
  margin: var(--space-1) 0 0;
  color: var(--color-ink-soft);
}

// ── Grid ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
.dev-grid {
  padding: 12px 8px 8px;
}

ion-col {
  padding: 6px;
}

// ── Developer Card ────────────────────────────────────────
.dev-card {
  border-radius: 18px;
  box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
  margin: 0;
}

.dev-card-content {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 14px;
  padding: 24px 16px 20px;
  text-align: center;
}

.dev-avatar {
  width: 72px;
  height: 72px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  /* Clips the photo to the circle. Without it a square upload shows its corners
     outside the disc, and the initials branch has no equivalent problem — which
     is exactly why the omission is invisible until someone uploads a photo. */
  overflow: hidden;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* Covers the same disc as .dev-initials, so the card is one size whether a
   person has a photo or not. `cover` rather than `contain` so an off-centre
   upload is cropped to the face instead of shrinking to a letterboxed stamp. */
.dev-photo {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  object-fit: cover;
  display: block;
}

.dev-initials {
  font-size: 22px;
  font-weight: 800;
  /* Plum, not white: the five avatar discs are pastel tiles now, and white on
     them measures 1.58–1.9:1. Plum clears 6:1 on all five. */
  color: var(--color-ink);
  width: 72px;
  height: 72px;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  border-radius: 50%;
}

.dev-info {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 10px;
  width: 100%;
}

.dev-name {
  font-family: var(--font-display);
  font-size: 16px;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--color-ink);
  margin: 0;
  line-height: 1.3;
}

.dev-roles {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  justify-content: center;
  gap: 5px;
}

/*
  Role chips. Background AND foreground are both declared here, per tint.

  They used to be `[color]="getRoleColor(role)"` in the template, which handed Ionic
  one name and let it pick both halves. The measured result on the live page was
  five unreadable chips — the label and the pill behind it landing within a few
  percent of the same lightness (1.11:1 to 3.67:1). `color` is built for tints, not
  for text contrast.

  Each foreground is `--color-ink`, which `check-contrast.mjs` already asserts
  against every one of these surfaces at 4.5:1 or better, so these pairs are guarded
  by the existing gate rather than by a comment. The admin credits page styles its
  identical chips the same way.

  `--tile-*` rather than the raw brand pastels: the tiles are the lighter surfaces
  intended for small text, and using them keeps the chips legible at 11px.
*/
.role-chip {
  font-size: 11px;
  height: 24px;
  margin: 0;
  --color: var(--color-ink);

  ion-label {
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
}

.role-lead {
  background: var(--tile-powder);
}

.role-dev {
  background: var(--tile-mint);
}

.role-design {
  background: var(--tile-blush);
}

.role-qa {
  background: var(--tile-lemon);
}

/* An unrecognised role gets a neutral chip rather than a hue of its own. */
.role-plain {
  background: var(--ion-color-light);
  border: 1px solid var(--color-border, #dfe4ec);
}

// ── Credit ────────────────────────────────────────────────
/* No border-top here, deliberately. This block sits directly above <app-footer>,
   which has its own hairline; two rules 8px apart read as a rendering fault
   rather than as two things. The credit is content, the footer's edge is
   chrome, and only the chrome gets an edge. */
.dev-footer {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 3px;
  padding: var(--space-4) 16px var(--space-3);
  text-align: center;
  margin: 8px 16px 0;
}
.footer-icon {
  --icon-size: 24px;
  color: var(--color-primary-ink);
}
.footer-text {
  font-size: 13px;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--color-ink);
  margin: 0;
}
.footer-sub {
  font-size: 12px;
  color: var(--ion-color-medium);
  margin: 0;
}
</file>

<file path="src/app/features/orders/orders.page.html">
<!-- src/app/features/orders/orders.page.html -->
<!-- Five-orites Scoop — Order History -->

<ion-header>
  <ion-toolbar color="primary">
    <ion-buttons slot="start">
      <ion-menu-button aria-label="Open navigation menu"></ion-menu-button>
    </ion-buttons>
    <ion-title>My Orders</ion-title>
    <ion-buttons slot="end">
      <app-cart-button></app-cart-button>
    </ion-buttons>
  </ion-toolbar>
</ion-header>

<ion-content fullscreen>
  <ion-refresher slot="fixed" (ionRefresh)="handleRefresh($event)">
    <ion-refresher-content></ion-refresher-content>
  </ion-refresher>

  <!-- Loading Skeleton -->
  @if (isLoading()) {
    <ion-list>
      @for (s of skeletonItems; track $index) {
        <ion-item class="order-skeleton-item">
          <div class="skeleton-wrap">
            <ion-skeleton-text animated style="height: 16px; width: 60%; margin-bottom: 6px"></ion-skeleton-text>
            <ion-skeleton-text animated style="height: 12px; width: 40%"></ion-skeleton-text>
          </div>
          <div slot="end">
            <ion-skeleton-text animated style="height: 24px; width: 80px"></ion-skeleton-text>
          </div>
        </ion-item>
      }
    </ion-list>
  }

  <!-- Error -->
  @if (!isLoading() && errorMessage()) {
    <app-empty-state
      icon="frown"
      title="Something went wrong"
      [message]="errorMessage()"
      actionLabel="Try again"
      (action)="loadOrders()"
    />
  }

  <!-- Empty -->
  @if (!isLoading() && !errorMessage() && orders().length === 0) {
    <app-empty-state
      icon="receipt"
      title="No orders yet"
      message="When you place an order, it'll show up here."
      actionLabel="Browse Flavors"
      (action)="goToProducts()"
    />
  }

  <!-- Orders List -->
  @if (!isLoading() && orders().length > 0) {
    <!--
      Paged at eight. This list was un-paged and unbounded — `getCustomerOrders`
      has no cap — so a customer with a few years of history got a single endless
      scroll with no way to jump. Eight a page is about one screen of this list,
      and the pager is the same component the admin screens use.

      The cancel button stays inside the row. `stopPropagation` on it is what
      keeps "Cancel Order" from also opening the tracker, which is the
      nesting-a-button-in-a-link case: the row is a routerLink and the button is
      a separate action on the same card.
    -->
    <div class="orders-body">
      @for (order of pagedOrders(); track trackOrder($index, order)) {
        <ion-item [routerLink]="['/orders', order.id]" detail="true" class="order-item" button>
          <div class="order-info">
            <div class="order-top-row">
              <span class="order-id">Order #{{ order.id.slice(-6).toUpperCase() }}</span>
              <app-order-status-badge [status]="order.status"></app-order-status-badge>
            </div>
            <p class="order-meta">
              {{ itemCount(order) }} item{{ itemCount(order) !== 1 ? 's' : '' }} &middot; {{ order.grandTotal | peso }}
              @if (order.voucherCode) {
                &middot; {{ order.voucherCode }}
              }
            </p>
            <p class="order-date">{{ formatDate(order.createdAt) }}</p>
            @if (canCancel(order)) {
              <ion-button
                fill="outline"
                color="danger"
                size="small"
                (click)="cancelOrder(order, $event)"
                [disabled]="cancellingId() === order.id"
                class="cancel-order-btn"
              >
                {{ cancellingId() === order.id ? 'Cancelling…' : 'Cancel Order' }}
              </ion-button>
            }
          </div>
        </ion-item>
      }

      <app-pagination
        [page]="page()"
        [total]="orders().length"
        [pageSize]="PAGE_SIZE"
        itemLabel="orders"
        (pageChange)="page.set($event)"
      />
    </div>
  }
  <app-footer></app-footer>
</ion-content>
</file>

<file path="src/app/features/products/product-detail/product-detail.page.scss">
// src/app/features/products/product-detail/product-detail.page.scss

.hero-skeleton {
  display: block;
  height: 260px;
}

.hero-wrap {
  position: relative;
  width: 100%;
  height: 260px;
  background: var(--color-brand-light);
  overflow: hidden;
}

.hero-image {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  object-fit: cover;
}

.hero-overlay {
  position: absolute;
  bottom: 12px;
  left: 14px;
}

.set-badge-hero {
  background: var(--color-brand-primary);
  /* Plum on the powder fill: 7.20:1. */
  color: var(--color-ink);
  font-size: 12px;
  font-weight: 600;
  padding: 4px 12px;
  border-radius: 20px;
}

.detail-body {
  padding: 20px 16px 40px;
}

.detail-name {
  font-size: 26px;
  font-weight: 800;
  color: var(--ion-color-dark);
  margin: 0 0 8px;
  letter-spacing: -0.5px;
}

.detail-description {
  font-size: 15px;
  color: var(--ion-color-medium);
  line-height: 1.6;
  margin: 0 0 24px;
}

.section-title {
  font-size: 18px;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--ion-color-dark);
  margin: 0 0 12px;
}

// ── Size Grid ────────────────────────────────────────────
.size-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  gap: 10px;
  margin-bottom: 24px;
}

.size-tile {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 4px;
  padding: 14px 10px;
  border-radius: 14px;
  border: 2px solid var(--ion-color-light-shade);
  background: #fff;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: all 0.15s ease;
  text-align: center;

  &.selected {
    border-color: var(--color-primary-ink);
    background: var(--color-brand-light);
  }

  &.out-of-stock {
    opacity: 0.5;
    cursor: not-allowed;
  }
}

.tile-size {
  font-size: 13px;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--ion-color-dark);
}

.tile-price {
  font-size: 18px;
  font-weight: 800;
  // Plum, and specifically NOT primary-ink: this price sits on a MINT tile, where
  // primary-ink is 4.09:1 - a fail. Measured 1.03:1 here, powder on mint. Plum is
  // 7.41:1 on mint and 12.88:1 on the white tiles beside it.
  color: var(--color-ink);
}

.tile-stock {
  font-size: 11px;
  font-weight: 500;
  // All three stock states take plum on the mint tile. tone-success-ink was the
  // obvious candidate and measures 4.28:1 there - still a fail. The state keeps its
  // colour on the tile and the icon; the TEXT takes the one value verified on every
  // surface this component sits on.
  &.stock-in-stock {
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  &.stock-low-stock {
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  &.stock-out-of-stock {
    color: var(--color-danger);
  }
}

// ── Quantity Row ─────────────────────────────────────────
.qty-row {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 12px;
  margin-bottom: 20px;
}

.qty-label {
  font-size: 15px;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--ion-color-dark);
}

/* One Lucide heart serves both states: .wished fills it with currentColor and
   switches the colour, replacing the ionicons heart / heart-outline pair that
   used to be swapped in via a template ternary. */
.wishlist-icon {
  --icon-size: 18px;
  transition: color 0.15s ease;
}
.wishlist-icon.wished {
  color: var(--ion-color-danger);
}
.wishlist-icon.wished svg {
  fill: currentColor;
}

.line-total {
  margin-left: auto;
  font-size: 20px;
  font-weight: 800;
  // 20px/800 is still not WCAG large text - that needs 24px. Plum rather than
  // primary-ink because this line sits on the mint summary tile.
  color: var(--color-ink);
}

// ── CTA ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
/* Plum, not white: white on the sunny accent measures 1.58:1. */
.add-to-cart-cta {
  --background: var(--color-brand-accent);
  --color: var(--color-ink);
  --border-radius: 14px;
  --padding-top: 16px;
  --padding-bottom: 16px;
  font-size: 16px;
  font-weight: 700;
  margin-bottom: 32px;
}

// ── Reviews ──────────────────────────────────────────────
.reviews-section {
  border-top: 1px solid var(--ion-color-light-shade);
  padding-top: 20px;
}

.reviews-empty {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 6px;
}

.no-reviews {
  font-size: 14px;
  color: var(--ion-color-medium);
  margin: 0;
}

.error-state {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  padding: 40px 20px;
  color: var(--color-danger);
}

/* The review LIST was in the template and in no stylesheet at all, so it rendered
   at the UA default: stars, author and comment all at body size with 1em of
   margin between them and nothing separating one review from the next.

   .review-author and .review-comment are the definitions from the moderation
   queue (admin/reviews) copied verbatim — the same review should not look like
   two different reviews because it is being read in a different place. */
.reviews-truncated {
  margin: 0 0 10px;
  font-size: 12px;
  color: var(--ion-color-medium);
}

.review-item {
  padding: 12px 0;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ion-color-light);
}

.review-author {
  margin: 4px 0 0;
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: 14px;
  color: var(--color-ink);
}

.review-comment {
  margin: 4px 0 0;
  font-size: 14px;
  line-height: 1.5;
  color: var(--color-ink);
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}

/* A text action, not a control. fill="clear" at size="small" keeps 0.9em of
   inline padding plus 2px of inline margin, which is what left "Withdraw"
   hanging a dozen pixels right of the review it belongs to. */
.withdraw-review-btn {
  --padding-start: 0;
  --padding-end: 0;
  margin-inline-start: 0;
}

/* Writing a review is a different job from reading them, so the form opens its
   own block instead of continuing the list's hairline rows. */
.review-form {
  margin-top: var(--space-5);
  padding-top: var(--space-4);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--ion-color-light-shade);
}

.review-form-note {
  margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
  font-size: 12px;
  color: var(--ion-color-medium);
}

/* An outline ion-button inherits --ion-color-primary, the powder brand hue: measured
   #8ECAE6 on this page's cream ground at 1.71:1. The one action on a product page
   that a returning customer reaches for was the least readable control on it. */
.wishlist-btn {
  --color: var(--color-primary-ink);
}
</file>

<file path="src/app/features/profile/profile.page.html">
<ion-header>
  <ion-toolbar>
    <ion-buttons slot="start">
      <ion-menu-button aria-label="Open navigation menu"></ion-menu-button>
    </ion-buttons>
    <ion-title>My Profile</ion-title>
  </ion-toolbar>
</ion-header>

<ion-content fullscreen>
  <div class="profile-wrap">
    @if (errorMessage()) {
      <app-alert-banner tone="danger" [message]="errorMessage()" />
    }

    <!--
      ONE container.

      This was four stacked sections — an avatar block with no surface at all, then
      three separate white cards each with 24px of padding, a 16px bottom margin
      and its own drop shadow. On a phone that is roughly 100px of card padding
      and 48px of margin between the cards, holding about 40px of actual content:
      the page was mostly the space between the boxes.

      Now there is one surface. The three groups are still visually separate — the
      avatar, then your details, then email, then notifications — but they are
      separated by hairline rules inside a single panel, which is the same
      information architecture at roughly a third of the height. The page also
      gets wider (720px rather than 640px), so the email and password fields stop
      being the narrowest thing on a desktop screen.

      The grouping is not decorative. Each section is a separate set of fields
      with its own save button and its own failure modes, and the dividers make
      it clear which button belongs to which group.
    -->
    <section class="profile-card">
      <!-- ── Avatar ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <header class="avatar-section">
        <div class="avatar-frame">
          @if (user()?.photoURL) {
            <img [src]="user()!.photoURL | cloudinary: 256" alt="Your profile photo" class="avatar-img" />
          } @else {
            <div class="avatar-initials" aria-hidden="true">{{ initials() }}</div>
          }
          @if (isUploadingPhoto()) {
            <div class="avatar-busy" aria-live="polite">
              <ion-spinner name="crescent"></ion-spinner>
            </div>
          }
        </div>

        <div class="avatar-copy">
          <span class="avatar-name">{{ user()?.displayName || 'Your profile' }}</span>
          <span class="avatar-email">{{ user()?.email }}</span>
        </div>

        <div class="avatar-actions">
          <!--
            A styled label wrapping a real <input type="file">, not a div with a
            click handler: that keeps it keyboard-reachable and screen-reader
            announceable, and gives us the native file picker for free.
          -->
          <label class="file-label" [class.disabled]="isUploadingPhoto()">
            <app-icon name="camera" />
            {{ isUploadingPhoto() ? 'Uploading…' : 'Change' }}
            <input
              type="file"
              accept="image/*"
              class="file-input"
              (change)="onPhotoSelected($event)"
              [disabled]="isUploadingPhoto()"
            />
          </label>

          @if (user()?.photoURL) {
            <button type="button" class="link-btn" (click)="removePhoto()">Remove</button>
          }
        </div>
      </header>

      <!-- ── Details ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <div class="group">
        <h2 class="panel-title">Your details</h2>

        <label class="field">
          <span class="field-label">Display name</span>
          <ion-input
            [ngModel]="displayName()"
            (ngModelChange)="displayName.set($event)"
            name="displayName"
            type="text"
            placeholder="Your name"
            maxlength="60"
            autocomplete="name"
          ></ion-input>
        </label>

        <label class="field">
          <span class="field-label">Phone number</span>
          <ion-input
            [ngModel]="phone()"
            (ngModelChange)="phone.set($event)"
            name="phone"
            type="tel"
            placeholder="0917 123 4567"
            inputmode="tel"
            autocomplete="tel"
          ></ion-input>
          <span class="field-hint">Used for delivery updates only.</span>
        </label>

        <ion-button
          expand="block"
          class="save-btn"
          [disabled]="!isDirty() || saveState() === 'saving'"
          (click)="saveProfile()"
        >
          @if (saveState() === 'saving') {
            <ion-spinner name="crescent" slot="start"></ion-spinner>
          }
          Save changes
        </ion-button>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Email ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <div class="group">
        <h2 class="panel-title">Email address</h2>

        @if (emailLocked()) {
          <!--
            A Google account's address belongs to Google. Changing it here would
            change the login identity and lock the user out, so it is shown, not
            hidden — hiding it looks like a missing feature.
          -->
          <div class="locked-note">
            <app-icon name="lock" />
            <div>
              <p class="locked-title">Managed by your Google account</p>
              <p class="locked-text">{{ user()?.email }}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        } @else {
          <label class="field">
            <span class="field-label">Email address</span>
            <ion-input
              [(ngModel)]="newEmail"
              name="newEmail"
              type="email"
              inputmode="email"
              autocomplete="email"
            ></ion-input>
          </label>

          <label class="field">
            <span class="field-label">Current password</span>
            <ion-input
              [(ngModel)]="emailPassword"
              name="emailPassword"
              type="password"
              autocomplete="current-password"
            ></ion-input>
            <!--
              Stated up front rather than discovered on submit: Firebase rejects an
              email change without a recent sign-in, and the resulting
              auth/requires-recent-login error names none of that.
            -->
            <span class="field-hint">Needed to confirm the change.</span>
          </label>

          <ion-button fill="outline" class="change-email-btn" [disabled]="isChangingEmail" (click)="changeEmail()">
            @if (isChangingEmail) {
              <ion-spinner name="crescent" slot="start"></ion-spinner>
            }
            Change email
          </ion-button>
          <p class="field-hint standalone-hint">
            We'll email a confirmation link to the new address before anything changes.
          </p>
        }
      </div>

      <!-- ── Password ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <!--
        Shown only to accounts that HAVE a password credential.

        The requirement this came from was "hide it for email sign-in, show it for
        anything else", which reads backwards: a Google-only account has no
        password at all, so it is the one case where the control cannot work, and
        an account carrying BOTH Google and email/password is the case where it
        must not be hidden. `hasPasswordProvider()` asks the question that actually
        decides it, from the same `providerData` the email-lock check uses.

        Stated rather than silently absent, so a Google-only user learns why there
        is no password to change instead of concluding the app has forgotten.
      -->
      @if (hasPasswordProvider()) {
        <div class="group">
          <h2 class="panel-title">Password</h2>

          <label class="field">
            <span class="field-label">Current password</span>
            <ion-input
              [(ngModel)]="currentPassword"
              name="currentPassword"
              type="password"
              autocomplete="current-password"
            ></ion-input>
          </label>

          <label class="field">
            <span class="field-label">New password</span>
            <ion-input
              [(ngModel)]="newPassword"
              name="newPassword"
              type="password"
              autocomplete="new-password"
            ></ion-input>
            <span class="field-hint">At least 6 characters.</span>
          </label>

          <label class="field">
            <span class="field-label">Confirm new password</span>
            <ion-input
              [(ngModel)]="confirmPassword"
              name="confirmPassword"
              type="password"
              autocomplete="new-password"
            ></ion-input>
          </label>

          <ion-button
            fill="outline"
            class="change-email-btn"
            [disabled]="isChangingPassword()"
            (click)="changePassword()"
          >
            @if (isChangingPassword()) {
              <ion-spinner name="crescent" slot="start"></ion-spinner>
            }
            Change password
          </ion-button>
        </div>
      } @else {
        <div class="group">
          <h2 class="panel-title">Password</h2>
          <div class="locked-note">
            <app-icon name="lock" />
            <div>
              <p class="locked-title">Managed by Google</p>
              <p class="locked-text">
                You sign in with Google, so this account has no password. Your password lives in your Google account
                settings.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      }

      <!-- ── Notifications ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <div class="group">
        <h2 class="panel-title">Order notifications</h2>

        <div class="toggle-row">
          <div class="toggle-text">
            <p class="toggle-title">Order updates</p>
            <p class="toggle-sub">Get a notification when your order's status changes.</p>
          </div>
          <ion-toggle
            [checked]="updatesOn()"
            (ionChange)="onNotificationsToggle($event)"
            aria-label="Order update notifications"
          ></ion-toggle>
        </div>

        <!--
          States the scope rather than describing a permission the app no longer
          asks for. The "Notifications are blocked" panel that used to sit here is
          gone: there is no OS permission behind an in-app notification any more,
          so the state it described is unreachable, and a control that can render
          a state it cannot escape is one that looks broken.
        -->
        <p class="toggle-hint">
          Notifications appear in this app &mdash; in your notifications list, and as a message while it is open.
          Nothing is sent while the app is closed.
          <a routerLink="/notifications" class="inline-link">Open your notifications</a>
        </p>
      </div>

      @if (saveState() === 'saved') {
        <p class="saved-note" role="status">Your profile has been updated.</p>
      }

      <!-- ── Delete account ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <!--
        Required by both app stores for any app that lets someone create an
        account, and it did not exist until this was added -- PRIVACY.md had to
        describe a manual email route because nothing could delete anything.

        Shown only to non-staff. `deleteAccount` refuses for a staff role anyway,
        because an owner deleting themselves would strand every admin page with
        no way back, so hiding it is better than offering it and failing.

        The copy is explicit about what happens to orders, because that is the
        part people hesitate over. The orders themselves stay -- they are the
        shop's accounting records -- but the name, email, address and notes on
        them are redacted. Saying "everything is deleted" when the address is
        still on last month's order would be a lie in the one place someone is
        trusting you.
      -->
      @if (!isStaff()) {
        <div class="group danger-group">
          <h2 class="panel-title">Delete account</h2>

          <p class="danger-copy">Permanently deletes your account and signs you out. This cannot be undone.</p>
          <p class="danger-copy">
            Your past orders are kept as the shop's sales records, but your name, email address and delivery address are
            removed from them.
          </p>

          @if (deleteError()) {
            <p class="danger-error" role="alert">{{ deleteError() }}</p>
          }

          @if (needsPasswordForDelete()) {
            <ion-item lines="none">
              <ion-input
                label="Confirm your password"
                labelPlacement="stacked"
                type="password"
                autocomplete="current-password"
                [(ngModel)]="deletePassword"
                (ionChange)="deletePassword = $any($event.detail.value) ?? ''"
              ></ion-input>
            </ion-item>
          }

          <ion-button
            expand="block"
            color="danger"
            class="delete-account-btn"
            [disabled]="isDeleting() || (needsPasswordForDelete() && !deletePassword)"
            (click)="confirmDeleteAccount()"
          >
            {{ isDeleting() ? 'Deleting…' : 'Delete my account' }}
          </ion-button>
        </div>
      }
    </section>
  </div>
  <app-footer></app-footer>
</ion-content>
</file>

